1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,143
Coach: Recognising a reason means showing that you have understood why it matters.

2
00:00:05,143 --> 00:00:08,143
Coach: It does not require accepting the proposal.

3
00:00:08,143 --> 00:00:14,143
Coach: You can be honest about both a useful consideration and a concern that remains.

4
00:00:14,493 --> 00:00:18,779
Coach: The group is planning twenty minutes with two sample problems.

5
00:00:18,779 --> 00:00:21,779
Coach: They want to understand the key reasoning.

6
00:00:21,779 --> 00:00:28,208
Coach: Their tool can show a hint or a full worked answer in this fictional case,

7
00:00:28,208 --> 00:00:32,922
Coach: but its output is unverified and no plan has been adopted.

8
00:00:33,272 --> 00:00:37,558
Maya: An answer tool can save time when we get stuck.

9
00:00:37,558 --> 00:00:42,272
Maya: I would use it as soon as a problem becomes difficult.

10
00:00:42,622 --> 00:00:45,193
Coach: Time is relevant to this task.

11
00:00:45,193 --> 00:00:52,479
Coach: But a possible saving does not settle whether help should be used at the first difficult step.

12
00:00:52,479 --> 00:00:55,479
Coach: That timing can remain open for discussion.

13
00:00:55,829 --> 00:01:00,972
Coach: A phrase such as I see why time matters recognises the reason.

14
00:01:00,972 --> 00:01:06,115
Coach: I agree that we should use it immediately accepts the proposed action.

15
00:01:06,115 --> 00:01:09,972
Coach: These are different commitments, even if both sound friendly.

16
00:01:10,322 --> 00:01:16,751
Coach: Do not use agreement as a polite disguise for a concern you want to raise.

17
00:01:16,751 --> 00:01:21,036
Coach: Equally, do not add an acknowledgement you do not mean.

18
00:01:21,036 --> 00:01:24,465
Coach: A direct, reasoned objection can also be appropriate.

19
00:01:24,815 --> 00:01:29,101
Coach: Before comparing replies, consider what you can genuinely recognise here.

20
00:01:29,101 --> 00:01:32,530
Coach: The time limit is real within the scenario.

21
00:01:32,530 --> 00:01:37,244
Coach: Whether immediate tool use best serves understanding has not been established.

22
00:01:45,594 --> 00:01:49,880
Coach: For this exercise, consider a speaker who values saving time

23
00:01:49,880 --> 00:01:55,880
Coach: but worries that immediate help could leave too little room for an independent attempt.

24
00:01:55,880 --> 00:01:58,451
Coach: Which reply expresses both points honestly?

25
00:01:58,801 --> 00:02:01,372
Coach: The peer replies are authored possibilities.

26
00:02:01,372 --> 00:02:05,230
Coach: They illustrate what the wording introduces into this exchange,

27
00:02:05,230 --> 00:02:12,087
Coach: not a prediction of Maya’s feelings or a guarantee that one sentence will resolve the difference.

28
00:02:20,437 --> 00:02:21,723
You: Saving time matters.

29
00:02:21,723 --> 00:02:29,437
You: I am concerned that using the tool immediately could leave too little room to try a step ourselves.

30
00:02:29,787 --> 00:02:33,644
Maya: I want to avoid spending the whole session stuck.

31
00:02:33,644 --> 00:02:37,073
Maya: What kind of first attempt do you mean?

32
00:02:37,423 --> 00:02:40,423
Coach: This reply recognises a task-related reason

33
00:02:40,423 --> 00:02:43,852
Coach: and states a possible concern without claiming certainty.

34
00:02:43,852 --> 00:02:47,280
Coach: Maya asks about the alternative in this example.

35
00:02:47,280 --> 00:02:53,709
Coach: The difference is now clearer, but no method has been agreed or shown to work.

36
00:02:54,059 --> 00:02:58,345
Coach: The shared task gives a reason to care about time.

37
00:02:58,345 --> 00:03:02,631
Coach: It also gives a reason to examine opportunities for reasoning.

38
00:03:02,631 --> 00:03:06,059
Coach: Recognising one consideration does not remove the other,

39
00:03:06,059 --> 00:03:09,488
Coach: and neither settles the method on its own.

40
00:03:09,838 --> 00:03:14,124
Coach: You can say what you recognise and what you question.

41
00:03:14,124 --> 00:03:15,410
Coach: Keep both specific.

42
00:03:15,410 --> 00:03:21,410
Coach: Empty praise followed by an unrelated objection does not make the concern clearer merely

43
00:03:21,410 --> 00:03:23,124
Coach: because it sounds polite.

44
00:03:23,474 --> 00:03:24,760
Coach: Acknowledgement is optional.

45
00:03:24,760 --> 00:03:29,902
Coach: If the reason is already understood, a concise objection may be enough.

46
00:03:29,902 --> 00:03:32,902
Coach: The aim is an honest, relevant response,

47
00:03:32,902 --> 00:03:37,188
Coach: not a requirement to begin every disagreement with I agree.

48
00:03:37,538 --> 00:03:43,109
Coach: For the same assigned position, form a short reply in your own words.

49
00:03:43,109 --> 00:03:47,824
Coach: Recognise the time concern if useful, then make the reservation clear.

50
00:03:47,824 --> 00:03:51,681
Coach: Do not claim that immediate help guarantees no learning.

51
00:03:52,031 --> 00:03:54,174
Coach: Think first or speak privately.

52
00:03:54,174 --> 00:03:56,317
Coach: Nothing is recorded or assessed.

53
00:03:56,317 --> 00:04:00,174
Coach: The pause is a small opportunity, not a deadline;

54
00:04:00,174 --> 00:04:02,745
Coach: pause playback if you want longer.

55
00:04:11,095 --> 00:04:13,238
Coach: If you tried a reply,

56
00:04:13,238 --> 00:04:17,524
Coach: check whether it says what the assigned speaker actually means.

57
00:04:17,524 --> 00:04:21,381
Coach: If you did not rehearse, you can return later.

58
00:04:21,381 --> 00:04:25,238
Coach: Next we will make the concern itself more precise.
